        "div_2": "Tomatoes · support & airflow",
        "div_3": "Peppers · room to mature",
        "div_4": "Onions or early lettuce",
        "div_5": "Basil near a few plants",
        "span_10": "4 feet",
        "p_4": "32 square feet. Diagram not to scale; zone sizes and plant counts are not calculated. “Back” means the taller side of your arrangement—not a compass direction. Match placement to your sunlight and access.",
        "span_11": "The selected direction",
        "h3_1": "Tomato & pepper",
        "p_5": "A mix built around tomatoes and peppers, with low crops at the reachable front. Check mature size and spacing for your varieties.",
        "a_8": "Try the interactive planner ",
        "span_12": "↗",
        "p_6": "The planner supplies a rough sketch too. It does not certify spacing for your varieties.",
        "p_7": "Other source concepts: a salad bed with greens, roots and herbs; a salsa bed with tomatoes, peppers, onions and cilantro; or a mixed bed with a main crop, roots and a leafy front strip.",
        "span_13": "Before anything goes in",
        "h2_2": "Fewer big plants.",
        "em_3": "More useful room.",
        "span_14": "01",
        "h3_2": "Start with size.",
        "p_8": "Zucchini can dominate. Cucumbers need support or sprawling room. Mature size matters more than the size of a starter pot.",
        "span_15": "02",
        "h3_3": "Keep access.",
        "p_9": "Can you reach the middle, supports and fruit? A productive-looking map is not useful if harvesting becomes a wrestling match.",
        "span_16": "03",
        "h3_4": "Respect the edges.",
        "p_10": "Pumpkins, winter squash and sprawling melons usually need more room. An edge experiment is different from a reliable fit.",
        "span_17": "The small support crew",
        "h2_3": "Herbs fit best",
        "em_4": "with a purpose.",
        "figcaption_2": "Basil reference photography · Alexas Fotos / Pexels",
        "h3_5": "Choose a few you’ll cut.",
        "p_11": "Try basil near tomatoes or peppers, narrow chives, reachable parsley, and thyme on drier edges. These are starting ideas, not a reason to fill every spare inch.",
        "h3_6": "Keep the collectors elsewhere.",
        "p_12": "Mint belongs in its own pot in this plan. Give a large rosemary or lavender plant room of its own rather than treating it as a tiny permanent tuck-in.",
